own to few cards, the best strategy is often to **play small to let your teammate get the lead**, especially if they might have a winning hand. However, my teammate passed last round after playing a single, indicating weakness.\n\nAlternatively, using a relatively high but not top card like ♣Q or ♦2 as a single might force the landlord to spend a high card, helping my teammate later.\n\nHowever, the safest and most cooperative move is to **play a low single** like ♠3 — it doesn\'t waste high cards, and if my teammate has a slightly higher card, they can take control and potentially run their hand.\n\nBut wait — the last active play was Black Joker by me, followed by two passes. So yes, this is indeed a free play. Now, instead of hoarding high cards, I should try to **get rid of unpaired, medium-high singles** that I cannot use in combinations, especially since I have no support for sequences.\n\nThe card ♦2 is very high — highest single after the red joker — so playing it now would waste it unless necessary.\n\nSimilarly, ♣Q is mid-high; there are likely higher singles still out (A, K, 2), so it\'s safe to play now only if aiming to clear.\n\nBest option: Play a **low unpaired card** to clean my hand without wasting power. That would be ♠3.\n\nAlternatively, play the **pair of jacks**, which is a decent-sized pair that might win the trick, letting me continue next time if others pass. Given that the landlord has only 3 cards left, gaining immediate control could allow me to play another round and possibly go out first.\n\nGiven that I have no further support for pairs beyond the jacks, and holding onto them may not help later, **now is the time to play them** while the table is open.\n\nAlso, my teammate already passed twice recently — they may not have a strong lead. So relying on them taking control soon may not be realistic.\n\nThus, playing **♥J ♦J** offers a chance to gain initiative, potentially play again, and deplete my hand faster.\n\nReason: Playing the pair of jacks gives me a chance to retain control. With both peasants having few cards, seizing the lead now increases the chance that one of us can empty our hand before the landlord. The landlord likely holds high cards, but if they don’t have a pair of aces or kings, they may be forced to pass. This aggressive move maximizes pressure and coordination opportunity.\n\nAnswer: {"cards": ["♥J", "♦J"]}'}
